::STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Set A has median 12 and range 4. Set B has median 12 and range 20. Which set is more consistent?
{
=A. Set A#The same median with smaller range means Set A has less spread.
~B. Set B#Set B has a larger range, so it is less consistent.
~C. Both are equally consistent#The ranges are different.
~D. Cannot compare because medians match#Matching medians still allow comparison of variability.
}

::STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Which summary is usually less affected by a high outlier?
{
=A. Median#The median depends on position, so one high value has less effect.
~B. Mean#The mean uses every value and can be pulled upward.
~C. Range#The range can increase greatly because of the high value.
~D. Maximum#The maximum is the high outlier itself.
}

::STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Store A wait times are 3, 4, 4, 5, 18. What value is the likely outlier?
{
~A. 3#3 is near the cluster.
~B. 4#4 is part of the cluster.
~C. 5#5 is near the cluster.
=D. 18#18 is far from the cluster of values between 3 and 5.
}

::STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Two classes have similar means, but Class B has much larger standard deviation. What is true?
{
~A. Class B has a smaller mean#The means are described as similar.
~B. Class B has no outliers#A larger standard deviation does not prove there are no outliers.
=C. Class B scores are more spread out#Larger standard deviation means values are typically farther from the mean.
~D. Class B must have fewer students#Standard deviation describes spread, not sample size.
}

::STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Why should outliers be considered when comparing data sets?
{
~A. They always make the median invalid#The median is often resistant to outliers.
=B. They can affect summaries and conclusions#Outliers can change measures such as mean and range and influence interpretation.
~C. They should always be deleted#Outliers should be investigated, not automatically removed.
~D. They only occur in categorical data#Outliers are unusual numerical values.
}
